  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1143 AM MST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nHigh pressure will begin to build and shift over eastern Idaho\nand western Wyoming through the rest of the week. Temperatures\nare still expected to be in the 50s into the low/mid 60s\nstarting tomorrow. We will need to watch for inversions to\npotentially hold temperatures back a bit, which is not always\nmodeled well with the Blend of Models. The air is pretty dry, so\nthe potential for fog or stratus is really limited unless we\nmaximize strong cooling overnight. By Friday, the high slips\nenough to the east to allow southerly flow to develop across\neastern Idaho. We will likely see some downslope conditions,\nwhich would potentially boost temperatures a bit as well. \n\nBy Friday evening/night through Saturday, we will see the first\nsystem swing across the state. We are going to be in a split\nflow pattern, which will take the main part of this storm and\ndevelop a closed low to our south. It does appear, though, we\nstill get a decent shot of moisture in the central mountains and\nhighlands closer to the Utah border. It isn't much, 0.10-0.30\" \nin favored areas (Sawtooths and surrounding mountains) and the \nSouth Hills & southeast highlands. Snow levels will be down\nbetween 6000-7000ft, generally up to 3\" where it is cold enough\nfor snow to fall and continually accumulate. There should be a \nlull in showers Sunday, before the next split flow system moves \ninland. Expect rain and snow to pick back up starting Monday.",
